# contrib-known-failures.allow — ratchet ledger for upstream's per-contrib
# regression suites. Companion: regress/run-contrib-regress-docker.sh and
# scripts/rowsort_compare.py --allow-file. Precedent:
# regress/known-failures.allow, crates/_support/seams_init/tests/*.allow.
#
# Semantics are the core suite's: a listed module may fail without failing CI;
# an unlisted module that fails breaks the build; a listed module that starts
# PASSING is stale and also breaks the build. The file can only shrink.
#
# SEED: 2026-09-04 full sweep, vendored suites at REL_18_3
# (62d6c7d3df6287f1bd83199c1a746e50d31571a0) against pgrust built from
# main @ 4ea755227a. Result: 33 passed, 5 failed, 12 skipped, 0 hard failures.
#
# 2026-09-05: hstore removed (now 34 passed, 4 failed). Its parser kept a local
# copy of scansup.c's scanner_isspace that omitted \v (0x0b), so
# E'key\u000B=>value\u000B'::hstore left the vertical tab inside the key and
# value instead of skipping it as surrounding whitespace. The \x0B seen in the
# diff was psql rendering that raw byte (mbprint.c pg_wcsformat), not an
# escaping bug in hstore's output — cpw already matches C exactly.
#
# NOT in this file, deliberately: the 12 skipped modules. pgrust has no
# extension ABI (crates/backend/utils/fmgr/dfmgr: "probin is a registry KEY,
# never a file: no C ABI exists to dlopen"), so an unported module reports
# C's file-not-found and the runner classifies it SKIP. Recording those as
# "known failures" would misrepresent an absent feature as a defect.

# --- genuine pgrust defects -------------------------------------------------

# citext/create_index_acl: "ERROR: permission denied for schema s" where
# upstream succeeds. citext's own tests pass; this is the ACL path. Same
# territory as the core suite's privileges/rowsecurity failures.
citext

# postgres_fdw: "use_remote_estimate is not yet supported (phase 3)" — an
# explicitly unported feature, self-reported by the code. Largest diff in the
# sweep (~11.8k lines) because it aborts early and everything after diverges.
postgres_fdw

# test_decoding: "ERROR: could not open file \"base/16708/2679\"" during
# logical decoding, with wal_level=logical correctly applied by the runner.
# Reads as a real logical-decoding defect, not a config gap.
test_decoding

# --- NOT a defect: unported archive_library ---------------------------------

# basic_archive needs archive_library = 'basic_archive', which pgrust does not
# implement. Unlike a LOAD failure this is SILENT — the server simply never
# archives — so the runner's not-ported detector (which keys off "could not
# access file") cannot see it. The whole diff is one row: the test asks whether
# an archived segment matching ^[0-9A-F]{24}$ exists and gets f instead of t.
# Listed so CI is green, annotated so nobody chases it as a bug.
basic_archive
